WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.759
For the next few minutes, we'll dig into task decomposition,

2
00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:08.159
the skill of breaking complex goals into structured subtasks.

3
00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:13.119
We'll cover why decomposition matters, how to analyze goals for natural boundaries,

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.479
what makes a subtask well-defined, and the real cost of over-decomposition.

5
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.479
This is one of the core architectural skills for anyone building agentic systems, so let's dive in.

6
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.040
Hand an agent a goal, like build me a competitive analysis, and it has to guess at everything.

7
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.959
Task decomposition is the fix. It's the process of breaking a high-level goal

8
00:00:36.959 --> 00:00:41.840
into smaller subtasks that agents or tools can actually execute.

9
00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.560
Each place where one subtask ends and the next begins is a handoff point,

10
00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:50.000
where one step's output becomes the next step's input,

11
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.319
and that boundary needs an explicit schema rather than a verbal description.

12
00:00:54.959 --> 00:00:57.759
Holding the whole thing together is the orchestrator,

13
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:02.400
the controlling agent that owns the master plan, assigns subtasks,

14
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.199
manages execution order, and synthesizes the pieces into a final result.

15
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.919
That structure is what turns a vague goal into work an agent can do.

16
00:01:13.279 --> 00:01:18.160
Why does decomposition matter so much? Because agents can't infer structure from

17
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.239
an underspecified goal. If you hand an agent a complex, monolithic task with no breakdown,

18
00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:30.000
it has to guess at boundaries, guess at priorities, and guess at what done even looks like.

19
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.760
When that fails, you have to retry the whole thing from scratch.

20
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.199
When you decompose the goal into subtasks, each piece becomes independently retriable,

21
00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:44.800
delegatable, and testable. You can also target error recovery precisely.

22
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.760
If step 4 fails, you retry step 4, not steps 1 through 3, again.

23
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.839
That's the real reliability gain, not smarter agents,

24
00:01:53.839 --> 00:01:56.559
but better structured work flowing through the system.

25
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.040
There's a spectrum here, and both ends cause problems.

26
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.839
On one end, monolithic tasks are simple to assign, but fragile.

27
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.160
One failure takes down the whole workflow, and there's no way to retry just the part that broke.

28
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.119
On the other end, over-decomposed tasks have too many steps,

29
00:02:17.119 --> 00:02:20.960
each one tiny, with coordination logic tying them together.

30
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.559
You end up spending more resources managing the workflow than doing the actual work.

31
00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:30.559
The latency of passing results through 10 handoffs

32
00:02:30.559 --> 00:02:34.960
can exceed the cost of just running a single, well-scoped agent.

33
00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.119
The right approach is somewhere in the middle, subtasks that are meaningful,

34
00:02:39.119 --> 00:02:44.720
executable units of work, not single-function microsteps that should have been combined.

35
00:02:45.919 --> 00:02:48.240
Let's compare these two approaches directly.

36
00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.360
A monolithic task is simple to hand off.

37
00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.639
You write one prompt, get one result, but it's hard to debug because the whole thing is one

38
00:02:56.639 --> 00:03:02.720
opaque unit. It's impossible to parallelize. And it fails completely if any part goes wrong,

39
00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.639
meaning you retry the entire task, even if only one step broke.

40
00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.160
A decomposed task has more upfront design work. You need to define subtasks,

41
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.160
their inputs, their outputs, and the handoff schemas between them.

42
00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.039
That's real investment, but each piece is independently retryable.

43
00:03:21.039 --> 00:03:25.679
And you can route different subtasks to agents with different specializations,

44
00:03:25.679 --> 00:03:29.919
such as a retrieval agent, an analysis agent, and a generation agent.

45
00:03:30.559 --> 00:03:33.839
The overhead is real, but so is the resilience you get in return.

46
00:03:34.639 --> 00:03:39.520
Before you decompose anything, you need to analyze the goal's structure.

47
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.960
There are three questions worth asking. First, can any part of this goal run independently,

48
00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:50.000
without needing output from another part? If yes, you have a candidate for parallel

49
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:56.559
decomposition. Second, does one step's output feed directly into the next step's input?

50
00:03:56.559 --> 00:04:00.160
That's a sequential dependency chain, and it has to stay sequential.

51
00:04:01.119 --> 00:04:04.880
Third, does the goal require distinctly different capabilities,

52
00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.240
like retrieval, then analysis, then generation?

53
00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.960
Each capability boundary is a natural decomposition point.

54
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:16.640
These three questions give you a structural map of the goal

55
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.679
before you write a single line of orchestration code.

56
00:04:19.679 --> 00:04:22.799
It's worth spending time on this upfront analysis,

57
00:04:22.799 --> 00:04:27.440
because the wrong decomposition design is expensive to fix after you've built the workflow.

58
00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:32.239
So what makes a subtask well-defined? Four things.

59
00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:37.440
First, single responsibility. One thing it does, one output it produces.

60
00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:43.200
Subtasks that try to do two jobs at once are harder to test, and harder to retry cleanly.

61
00:04:43.839 --> 00:04:49.359
Second, defined inputs. The subtask knows exactly what context it receives,

62
00:04:49.359 --> 00:04:54.000
and doesn't rely on ambient state, from a prior conversation it wasn't part of.

63
00:04:54.959 --> 00:04:59.200
Third, bounded scope. Small enough that retrying it on failure

64
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.119
doesn't force you to rerun the entire workflow from the beginning.

65
00:05:03.119 --> 00:05:08.000
And fourth, clean structured output, that the orchestrator can route directly,

66
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:13.679
without parsing freeform text. If a subtask fails any of these four criteria,

67
00:05:13.679 --> 00:05:18.239
it'll cause reliability problems downstream that are hard to trace back to the source.

68
00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.200
Handoff points are where most agentic workflows break down in practice.

69
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:26.799
A handoff is the boundary between two subtasks,

70
00:05:26.799 --> 00:05:30.239
where one agent's output becomes another agent's input.

71
00:05:30.239 --> 00:05:34.799
The first key principle is to pass only what the next step actually needs.

72
00:05:35.359 --> 00:05:40.000
Don't dump your entire context window into the handoff payload,

73
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.440
because that creates noise and can cause the receiving agent to focus on irrelevant information.

74
00:05:45.839 --> 00:05:50.559
Use structured schemas, not freeform text,

75
00:05:50.559 --> 00:05:54.640
so the receiving agent doesn't have to interpret ambiguous output.

76
00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.720
And document what isn't passed.

77
00:05:57.279 --> 00:06:01.679
Subagents don't automatically have access to everything the orchestrator knows.

78
00:06:01.679 --> 00:06:04.480
If you don't explicitly document those gaps,

79
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:08.000
subagents make false assumptions about what they have,

80
00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.720
and that causes silent errors that are very hard to debug after the fact.

81
00:06:12.959 --> 00:06:16.079
Every boundary you add to a workflow has a cost.

82
00:06:16.079 --> 00:06:19.040
More subtasks means more orchestration logic,

83
00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.880
more latency between steps, and more potential failure points.

84
00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.000
Each handoff is a place where context can be lost.

85
00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.320
A schema mismatch can cause an error,

86
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:32.559
or a partial failure can leave your workflow in an inconsistent state.

87
00:06:32.559 --> 00:06:37.519
Deeply decomposed systems are also harder to trace when something goes wrong.

88
00:06:37.519 --> 00:06:40.880
You're debugging a pipeline, not a single agent response.

89
00:06:41.839 --> 00:06:45.279
The tradeoff is reliability versus complexity.

90
00:06:45.279 --> 00:06:48.239
Decompose enough to get the reliability benefits,

91
00:06:48.239 --> 00:06:53.040
but stop before you're spending more energy managing the workflow than executing it.

92
00:06:53.839 --> 00:06:57.679
Decomposition is really a question about where work should split.

93
00:06:58.239 --> 00:07:00.720
The boundary itself is what you're designing.

94
00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.480
Every boundary has to earn its keep.

95
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.359
Each one buys you a retry point.

96
00:07:05.359 --> 00:07:07.920
Each one also costs you coordination effort.

97
00:07:08.559 --> 00:07:12.959
Get that trade right and the whole workflow stays traceable when something breaks.

98
00:07:13.679 --> 00:07:15.040
That's it for this lesson.

99
00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:16.320
I'll see you in the next one.

